# README – Historical Marginal Genetic Contribution Sensitivity Analysis
## Updated analysis using 1998 as the studbook-closure breakpoint

## 1. Purpose

This README documents the updated historical marginal genetic contribution sensitivity analysis for the South African Boerperd pedigree.

This is the version in which the three reference populations were:

1. 1973 cohort
2. 1944–1997
3. 1998–2024

The historical sensitivity scenarios:

- S1 = 29 animals
- S2 = 39 animals
- S3 = 343 animals

The aim was to determine how much marginal genetic contribution from the historical pre-1973 pedigree persisted into later populations, and whether that estimate changed depending on how the historical population was defined.

---

## 2. Main analytical question

The central question was:

> How much marginal genetic contribution from animals representing the historical pre-1973 population is retained in later South African Boerperd reference populations, and how sensitive is that estimate to the way the historical population is defined?

The analysis was therefore designed as a sensitivity analysis rather than as an attempt to claim that one historical definition was unquestionably correct.

---

## 3. Why a sensitivity analysis was necessary

The oldest part of the pedigree contains incomplete birth-year information.

This causes two problems:

- confirmed ancestors of the 1973 cohort can have unknown birth years; and
- additional animals with known pre-1973 birth years occur in the pedigree even if they were not selected specifically through the 1973 cohort.

A single strict historical definition could therefore exclude potentially relevant animals or depend too heavily on one cohort.

For this reason, three increasingly inclusive historical sets were compared.

If S1, S2 and S3 give similar results, the historical contribution estimate is relatively robust to the chosen definition.

If they differ strongly, the result is sensitive to how the incomplete early pedigree is defined.

This sensitivity logic is a study-specific design choice, not a requirement imposed by visPedigree.

---

## 4. Historical scenarios

### S1 – Restricted known-date ancestors

S1 contained 29 animals that:

- were confirmed ancestors of the 1973 cohort; and
- had a known birth year before 1973.

This was the most restrictive historical definition.

Why it was used:
S1 represented animals that could both be placed before 1973 from recorded birth-year information and be linked genealogically to the 1973 cohort.

Limitation:
S1 excludes confirmed ancestors of the 1973 cohort when their birth year is unknown.

---

### S2 – S1 plus confirmed ancestors with unknown birth years

S2 contained 39 animals:

- the 29 animals in S1; plus
- 10 additional confirmed ancestors of the 1973 cohort whose birth years were unknown.

Why it was used:
S2 tested whether missing birth-year information materially changed the estimated historical contribution.

The key S1 versus S2 question was:

> Does including confirmed ancestors of the 1973 cohort whose birth dates are unknown materially change the result?

If S1 and S2 are similar, those missing birth dates have relatively little effect on the estimate.

If they differ substantially, uncertainty in the early birth-year records matters.

---

### S3 – Broad recorded pre-1973 set

S3 contained 343 animals with:

- a known birth year; and
- birth year before 1973.

These animals did not have to be selected specifically because they were ancestors of the 1973 cohort.

Why it was used:
S3 tested whether using a broader pre-1973 historical set changed the estimated contribution compared with restricting the historical pool to animals linked specifically to the 1973 cohort.

Important wording:
S3 should not be called the complete historical genetic base of the breed.

A safer description is:

> S3 represented the broadest pre-1973 historical set identifiable from animals with recorded birth years in the available pedigree.

The 343 animals are therefore an analytical historical set, not a historical census estimate.

---

## 5. Relationship among the scenarios

The expected historical-pool sizes were:

S1 = 29  
S2 = 39  
S3 = 343

The scripts also checked that:

- S1 was contained within S2; and
- S1 was contained within S3.

These are not three biological populations. They are three alternative analytical definitions of the uncertain historical population.

---

## 6. Reference populations in the updated analysis

### Reference population 1 – 1973 cohort

All animals born in 1973.

This cohort was retained as a specific reference population because it marks the formal historical boundary used in the study.

### Reference population 2 – 1944–1997

All animals born from 1944 through 1997, inclusive.

This represents the recorded pedigree period before the 1998 studbook-closure breakpoint used in the study.

Importantly, this population overlaps with:

- some pre-1973 historical animals; and
- the 1973 cohort itself.

That overlap was handled deliberately rather than forcing every animal into one mutually exclusive analytical group.

### Reference population 3 – 1998–2024

All animals born from 1998 through 2024, inclusive.

This was the post-closure reference population.

Animals born in 2025 or later were excluded.

---

## 7. Why 1998 was used

The updated analysis used 1998 as the breakpoint because the project treated 1998 as the year in which the South African Boerperd studbook closed.

This allowed the analysis to compare a pre-1998 period with a 1998–2024 post-closure reference population.

Important caution:

The analysis can show that marginal historical contribution differs between reference periods.

It cannot by itself prove that any change was caused by studbook closure.

A defensible statement is:

> Historical contribution was compared between populations defined before and from the 1998 studbook-closure breakpoint.

Any causal interpretation requires additional evidence.

---

## 8. Why the reference populations stayed fixed across S1, S2 and S3

For a sensitivity analysis, the factor deliberately changed was the definition of the historical pool.

The target/reference animals were therefore held constant.

Within a reference period:

S1 vs S2 vs S3

tests the effect of the historical-pool definition.

If the reference population also changed, differences could not be attributed clearly to S1, S2 or S3.

This was a project-specific analytical design choice.

---

## 9. Important overlap issue in the 1944–1997 reference population

The complete 1944–1997 reference population overlaps with:

- the 1973 cohort; and
- some animals in the historical pre-1973 sets.

A CFC-style grouping system permits only one numeric group assignment per animal.

Therefore, the complete 1944–1997 reference population could not be reconstructed accurately from a single CFC group.

To solve this, complete reference-population ID lists were exported and used directly in the visPedigree analysis:

reference_IDs_1973.csv  
reference_IDs_1944_1997.csv  
reference_IDs_1998_2024.csv

This ensured that reference-population membership was defined correctly even where analytical groups overlapped.

---

## 10. Pedigree used

The marginal contribution calculation used the complete, loop-resolved and uncut pedigree.

The underlying animal–sire–dam relationships were retained.

Only the historical-pool definition changed between S1, S2 and S3.

---

## 11. Why the pedigree was kept complete

The question concerned how historical animals contributed through the recorded genealogy to later reference populations.

For that reason, the ancestral pathways linking ancestors and descendants needed to remain available to the contribution algorithm.

The safest wording is:

> Historical groups were defined using birth year and ancestry, while the complete recorded pedigree structure was retained for marginal-contribution calculation.

Avoid saying that truncating the pedigree would create incorrect founders.

A more precise statement is:

> Truncating the pedigree changes the pedigree base and removes ancestral relationships outside the retained pedigree.

Because the analysis aimed to measure contribution through the available recorded genealogy, the pedigree was left uncut.

---

## 12. Preparing the pedigree in visPedigree

The complete loop-resolved pedigree was converted to a tidyped object in the R package visPedigree.

The analysis used the general form:

```r
ped_tidy <- tidyped(
  ped_input,
  addgen = TRUE,
  addnum = TRUE
)
```

This prepared the pedigree for the contribution analysis.

For manuscript wording, it is sufficient to state:

> The complete loop-resolved pedigree was converted to a tidyped pedigree object in visPedigree before marginal ancestor contribution analysis.

---

## 13. Marginal ancestor contribution analysis

For each reference population, marginal ancestor contributions were calculated using:

```r
pedcontrib(
  ped = ped_tidy,
  reference = reference_ids,
  mode = "ancestor",
  top = nrow(ped_tidy)
)
```

This was run separately for:

- 1973 cohort
- 1944–1997
- 1998–2024

The returned ancestor tables were then used to calculate S1, S2 and S3 historical contributions.

---

## 14. Why mode = "ancestor" was used

The study question concerned historical ancestors rather than founder-only contributions.

Therefore:

```r
mode = "ancestor"
```

was used.

The ancestor-contribution method implemented in visPedigree follows the marginal ancestor framework associated with the probability-of-gene-origin approach described by Boichard and colleagues.

---

## 15. What marginal contribution means

Ancestors in a pedigree are not independent.

An older ancestor can contribute through a later influential descendant. If both were treated as independent full contributions, pedigree pathways would overlap.

The marginal ancestor procedure works iteratively and assigns an ancestor the additional contribution not already explained by more influential ancestors selected earlier.

For this study, marginal contribution was appropriate because the question concerned:

> the retained influence of historical ancestors while accounting for overlapping pedigree pathways.

This should not be phrased as marginal contribution being universally better than all other pedigree methods.

---

## 16. Why simple ancestor counts were not the main measure

A count tells us how many ancestors are represented.

It does not tell us how strongly they contributed.

One ancestor may have a large retained influence in the reference population, while another may have only a small marginal contribution.

Therefore, the main response variable was marginal genetic contribution rather than ancestor count alone.

---

## 17. Why top = nrow(ped_tidy) was used

The top argument controls how many high-ranking contributors are returned by pedcontrib().

The aim was to evaluate the contribution of the complete historical pool, including historical animals with small marginal contributions.

Therefore:

```r
top = nrow(ped_tidy)
```

was used so that the analysis was not restricted to a small top-ranked subset.

---

## 18. Main fields in the returned ancestor table

The ancestor output contained fields including:

Ind  
Contrib  
CumContrib  
Rank

For the historical-pool calculation, the key field was:

Contrib

because this represented the marginal contribution assigned to each returned ancestor.

---

## 19. How the contribution of S1, S2 and S3 was calculated

For each reference population:

1. Run pedcontrib() using the complete pedigree.
2. Obtain the returned marginal ancestor table.
3. Match the returned ancestor IDs to the S1, S2 or S3 ID list.
4. Retain the matched historical animals.
5. Sum their Contrib values.
6. Multiply by 100 to express the result as a percentage.

Conceptually:

```r
matched <- ancestor_table[
  ancestor_table$Ind %in% historical_ids,
  ,
  drop = FALSE
]

contribution <- sum(
  matched$Contrib,
  na.rm = TRUE
)

contribution_percent <- 100 * contribution
```

The historical-pool contribution can be represented as:

C(s,r) = sum of q(i,r) for all historical animals i in historical set H(s)

where:

- C(s,r) is the summed marginal contribution for scenario s in reference population r;
- H(s) is the historical animal set;
- q(i,r) is the marginal contribution of animal i to reference population r.

This summation step is our project-specific analysis of the pedcontrib() output.

---

## 20. Meaning of the final percentage

The final percentage represents:

> the summed marginal genetic contribution assigned to animals belonging to the defined historical pool for a specified reference population.

It is not:

- the percentage of all pedigree animals born before 1973;
- the proportion of all ancestors that were historical;
- the founder proportion;
- a census estimate; or
- a molecular genomic ancestry percentage.

It is a pedigree-based marginal contribution estimate.

---

## 21. Unmatched historical animals

Some S1, S2 or S3 animals may not appear as separate marginal contributors in a particular reference population's returned ancestor table.

The safest wording is:

> An unmatched historical animal did not appear as a separately returned marginal contributor in that reference population's ancestor table.

Possible reasons include:

- the animal was not ancestral to that reference population; or
- it did not retain a separate marginal contribution after overlapping ancestry was accounted for.

Do not automatically state that every unmatched animal had zero genetic importance without checking its pedigree position.

---

## 22. Contribution-total quality check

Because the analysis requested the full returned contributor table, the Contrib values were checked to ensure that the complete table summed to approximately one:

```r
sum(
  ancestor_table$Contrib,
  na.rm = TRUE
)
```

This served as a project quality-control check.

