## 33. Short Methods-style explanation

The complete loop-resolved South African Boerperd pedigree was retained for historical marginal contribution analysis. To evaluate sensitivity to incomplete early pedigree records, three alternative historical sets were defined. S1 comprised 29 confirmed ancestors of the 1973 cohort with known birth years before 1973; S2 additionally included 10 confirmed ancestors of the 1973 cohort with unknown birth years; and S3 comprised all 343 animals in the pedigree with known birth years before 1973. Marginal ancestor contributions were calculated using `visPedigree::pedcontrib()` for three fixed reference populations: the 1973 cohort, animals born from 1944–1997, and animals born from 1998–2024. Explicit reference-population ID lists were used because the 1944–1997 population overlapped with the 1973 cohort and with some historical animals. For each reference population, returned marginal ancestor IDs were matched to S1, S2 and S3, and the corresponding `Contrib` values were summed and expressed as percentages. The resulting scenario estimates were compared to evaluate the sensitivity of historical contribution estimates to the operational definition of the early pedigree population.

---

## 34. Scientific methodological basis

Boichard, D., Maignel, L., & Verrier, E. (1997).

The value of using probabilities of gene origin to measure genetic variability in a population.

Genetics Selection Evolution, 29, 5–23.

This paper provides the scientific basis for the probability-of-gene-origin and effective-ancestor framework underlying marginal ancestor contribution analysis.

---

## 35. Software

The analysis was performed using the R package:

visPedigree

with the function:

pedcontrib()

and:

mode = "ancestor"


